>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `add_split`

``` hol4
simpLib.add_split : thm -> simpset -> simpset
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Adds one named split rule to a simpset.

A call `add_split th ss` returns a copy of `ss` with a looper that calls
[`splitLib.SPLIT_TAC`](#splitLib.SPLIT_TAC) using `th`. The theorem must
be a named database theorem in the split-rule format documented there.
The input simpset is unchanged.

If the right side of the rule is syntactically a negation, `add_split`
installs an assumption looper named

``` hol4
split_asm thy$theorem
```

Otherwise it installs a conclusion looper named

``` hol4
split thy$theorem
```

Adding another looper with the same strategy name replaces the existing
one in its current position. The rule is used by simplification tactics,
whose looper pipeline repeatedly simplifies after successful splits. It
is not run by conversion-only interfaces such as `SIMP_CONV`.

Unlike the `[split]` attribute, `add_split` does not modify the persistent
split theorem set; its effect is confined to the returned simpset.

### Failure

Fails if `th` is not a well-formed split rule or has no local or stored
database name. Merely having no matching redex in a later goal is not an
error in `add_split` itself.

### Example

Given a stored theorem named `pickTheory$pick_split`:

``` hol4
Theorem pick_split:
  !P. P (pick b x y) <=>
      (b ==> P x) /\ (~b ==> P y)
Proof
  ...
QED

val pick_ss = simpLib.add_split pick_split bool_ss;

(* SIMP_TAC pick_ss [] can now split occurrences of pick. *)
```

